## ABSTRACT
Atomically precise gold (Au) nanoclusters (NCs) as visible light photosensitizers supported on the substrate for photoredox catalysis have attracted considerable attentions. However, efficient control of their photocatalytic activity and long-term stability is still challenging. Herein, we report a coordination-assisted self-assembly strategy in combination with electrostatic interaction to sandwich \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}(\mathsf{C a p t})_{18}\) (abbreviated as \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}\), Capt \(=\) captopril) NCs between an inner core and an outer shell mode of UiO-66, denoted as UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66. Notably, the sandwich-like nanocomposite displays significantly enhanced catalytic activity along with an excellent stability when used in the selective photocatalytic aerobic oxidation of sulfide to sulfoxide. As comparison, \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}\) NCs simply located at the outer surface or insider matrix of UiO-66 (short as \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}/\mathsf{U i O-66}\) and \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}@\mathsf{U i O-66}\)) show poor stability and low conversion, respectively. This structure regulated difference in the catalytic performances of three nanocomposites is assigned to the varied distribution of active sites (Au NCs) in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). This work offers the opportunity for application of nanoclusters in catalysis, energy conversion and even biology.
## KEYWORDS
Au nanoclusters, metal-organic frameworks, photocatalysis, singlet oxygen, sulfoxidation
## 1 Introduction
Atomically precise metal nanoclusters (NCs) with the precise composition and crystal structure have been broadly recognized as ideal candidates in catalysis [1–5] due to their intriguing features including ultra-small size, abundant active sites, large specific surface and unique electronic structures [6–8]. In addition, their well-defined atomic arrangement provides an excellent model for the study of correlation between structures and catalytic property [9, 10]. Unfortunately, the instability of NCs seriously impedes their fundamental and application studies in thermal, photo- or electro-catalysis [11]. Though many solid supports have been utilized to stabilize metal NCs for practical heterogeneous catalysis [12], these traditional oxides like \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{CeO}\), with weak metal–support interactions and low specific surface areas cannot efficiently avoid the loss or coalescence of the active Au NCs [11], thereby leading to the gradually decreased catalytic activity during reactions.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with extraordinarily large surface area, ordered pore structure, and easy tailorable chemistry are promising support materials [13–16]. Such characteristics not only strengthen immobilization of metal NCs through strong coordination interactions [17] (e.g. carboxyl groups on Au NCs interact with metal nodes of
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with extraordinarily large surface area, ordered pore structure, and easy tailorable chemistry are promising support materials [13–16]. Such characteristics not only strengthen immobilization of metal NCs through strong coordination interactions [17] (e.g. carboxyl groups on Au NCs interact with metal nodes of MOFs) or electrostatic interactions [18] (e.g. negatively charged Au NCs interact with positively charged metal nodes of MOFs), but also facilitate the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates or products through ordered pores [19]. Unfortunately, previous works are mainly focused on investigation of metal nanoparticles encapsulated into MOF-based scaffolds [20–22], i.e. representative "ship in bottle" [23] and "bottle around ship" [24] strategies, and the metal NCs-MOF based nanocomposites are rarely touched.
like nanocomposite exhibited higher catalytic activity, superior selectivity as well as good stability in photocatalytic aerobic oxidation of sulfide to sulfoxide by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), in comparison to the counterparts (\(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}-66\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\)) with the same amount of Au loading. A series of experiments, such as high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM), high-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS), Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) and electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra, indicate that the enhanced catalytic activity and stability are ascribed to the efficient mass transport inside porous MOFs and strong Au NCs–MOFs interface interactions.
\(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{Capt})_{18}\) (\(\mathrm{Capt}=\) captopril), one of the most studied NCs, was chosen as the investigated subject, since its high thermal stability [25] (Fig. S1 in the ESM), acid resistance [26] and rich carboxyl groups endow the high structural integrity as well as strong coordinative and electrostatic interactions during formation of Au NCs–UiO-66 nanocomposites. The high quality of as-prepared \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) nanoclusters is proved by matrix-assisted laser desorption ionization (MALDI), UV–Vis fingerprint absorption spectrum and HRTEM imaging (Figs. S2–S4 in the ESM). UiO-66 used in this work could be obtained under mild synthesis conditions [27]. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern (Fig. S5 in the ESM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) technique (Fig. S6 in the ESM) were performed to verify the successful synthesis of UiO-66. Three \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\)–UiO-66 nanocomposites with different structures were fabricated through strong interactions (coordinative and electrostatic adsorption) between carboxyl groups of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) and metal nodes of UiO-66 (detail is discussed in Fig. S7 in the ESM). As manifested in Scheme 1, \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}-66\) was prepared by simple immersing UiO-66 in the stock solution of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\). Meanwhile, intercalation of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) into UiO-66 matrix (\(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\)) was carried out by mixing \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) and UiO-66 precursors (solution of zirconium oxide clusters) before introducing terephthalic acid (PTA) ligands to grow MOFs around NCs. The synthesis of the UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66
was carried out through following the same experimental procedure of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) except using pre-prepared \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\) UiO-66 as the stock to replace \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\). The exact amount of Au NCs was determined by inductively coupled plasma (ICP) spectra (Table S1 in the ESM), suggesting that the loading content was identical for these three nanocomposites.
The morphology and geometrical structure of three nanocomposites were examined by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), TEM and EDS images (Fig. 1 and Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM). As demonstrated in Figs. 1(a) and 1(b), UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66 is of spherical shape with an average diameter of \(206~\mathrm{nm}\). Notably, the obvious sandwich structure of UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66 is distinguished both in HRTEM image (Fig. 1(c)) and HAADF-STEM image (Fig. 1(e)), which display a light-colored outer UiO-66 shell (highlighted by red lines) and a bright Au NC interlay (Fig. 1(e)). Moreover, there is nearly no Au present in the outer UiO-66 shell through comparing the element mapping of Au with Zr, O and C element (Fig. 1(d)). EDS element spectrum and relative content analysis are also shown in Fig. 1(f). All the results validate that the sandwich-like UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66 is successfully synthesized. In contrast, Au distribution inside \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) is quite uniform, which is reasonable that Au NCs homogeneously disperse in MOFs matrix (Fig. S8 in the ESM). And Au NCs anchor on the outer surface of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}-66\) sample (Fig. S9 in the ESM). As reported [15], there is no additional outer UiO-66 shell for both \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) (Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM).
The crystal nature of three \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\)–UiO-66 nanocomposites
was investigated by PXRD technique. There is no obvious difference (intensity or peak shift) in the PXRD patterns of three nanocomposites compared with pristine UiO-66, which is ascribed to the high crystallinity of MOFs, ultra-small size and low loading amount of Au NCs (Fig. S10 in the ESM). The BET surface area value of UiO-66, \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\), \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) and UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) is 1,150.5, 886, 981.1 and \(1,036.9\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively (Fig. S11 and Table S2 in the ESM). The surface area and the pore volume for all the nanocomposites are decreased compared with UiO-66, due to the loading of Au NCs. Of particular interest, the pore diameters of all the samples are nearly the same, indicating the size of Au NCs is too small to damage the pore structure. Therefore, it can be concluded that UiO-66 matrix retains its structural integrity for all the nanocomposites.
Sulfoxides are known to be intermediates in many important biologically active products [28]. However, it is a challenge to selective oxidation of organic sulfides to sulfoxides without overoxidation to sulfones [29]. Until now, various methods have been developed to solve this problem including photooxidation of sulfides promoted by \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) with photosensitizers [30] as well as selection of suitable reaction solvent [31] and mild oxidizing agent [32]. Herein, \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{Capt})_{18}\) is able to act as an excellent photosensitizer to produce \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), owing to its well-defined \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) adsorption sites and long lifetime of the electronic excited states (Fig. S12 in the ESM) [33]. So, we tested the performance of all three nanocomposites in selective photocatalytic oxidation of organic sulfide to sulfoxide [34], and Table 1 and Figs. S13–S16 in the ESM summarized the experimental results. It is noted that the photocatalytic oxidation of phenylmethyl sulfide does not happen using pristine UiO-66 as the catalysts (Table 1, entries 1) or under the dark condition (Table 1, entries 2, 6, and 10). \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) exhibit the conversion of \(97.5\%\) and \(46.9\%\) at room temperature
for \(10~\mathrm{h}\), respectively (Table 1, entries 3 and 11). This result indicates that the substrate diffusion and mass transport in \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) are largely restricted, caused by the fact that some active sites (Au NCs) penetrate deeply into UiO-66 matrix instead of anchoring on the out surface of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\). This hypothesis is further verified by the ESR experiments (detail is discussed later). As shown in Fig. 3(c), \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) displays the lowest yield of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) among three nanocomposites. Significantly, the sandwich-like UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) nanocomposite exhibits the remarkable activity with nearly full conversion of \(94.3\%\) under the same reaction condition (Table 1, entries 7) even protected by another outer UiO-66 shell. This excellent catalytic activity is attributed to the thinner UiO shell that facilitates the substrate diffusion and mass transport. The optimum thickness of UiO-66 shell for that sandwich-like catalyst is 10 to \(20~\mathrm{nm}\), by comparing with another two sandwich-like nanocomposites with thick outer MOF shells (detail is discussed in Figs. S17 and S18 in the ESM).
The selectivity and stability of the catalysts are another two key factors for assessing the catalytic performance. As for the first cycle, selectivity of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) toward sulfoxide is \(73.2\%\) and \(97.5\%\), respectively. The large selectivity difference might be elucidated by the stability of Au NCs. Only after one cycle of reaction, there are obvious large Au nanoparticles appear in \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) sample (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b)), and such coalescence of Au NCs into varied-sized Au nanoparticles gives rise to the reduced reaction selectivity [12]. On the contrary, there are no evident morphological change and Au NCs aggregation for \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) after three catalytic cycles (Fig. 2(c)). Impressively, sandwich-like UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) also maintains good stability, analogous to \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) (Fig. 2(d)). As a result, UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) nanocomposite shows an excellent reaction selectivity of \(91\%\) for sulfoxide. In addition, after three successive catalytic cycles, the selectivity of UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) still keeps to be \(90.8\%\). This catalytic performance comparison of three nanocomposites highlights the unique advantages of UiO- \(66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}{-66}\) as the catalysts, in another word, the thin shell coverage on Au NCs dramatically improves their stability whilst keeping high activity and selectivity.
To understand the reaction mechanism, the ESR spectra of all the Au–UiO-66 nanocomposites were recorded under dark or light irradiation using 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine (TEMP) as trapping agent (Fig. 3). As expected, all the Au-UiO-66 nanocomposites display enhanced ESR signal after light irradiation, indicating generation of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \). As comparison, the oxidation reaction does not happen under the dark condition (Table 1, entries 2, 6, and 10), revealing \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) originates from the reactive oxygen species (ROS) during the reaction. The mechanism of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) generation could be explained by the Dextertype electron exchange coupling between nanocomposites and \( ^{3}\mathrm{O}_{2} \), which is similar to the previous reported \( \mathrm{Au}_{38}\mathrm{S}_{2}(\mathrm{SAdm})_{20} \) system [29]. The detail of the spin flip and electron exchange for \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) generation is described in Fig. S19 in the ESM. Note that \( \mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{UiO}-66 \) possesses the highest yield of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) among three nanocomposites due to the absence of the outer layer of UiO-66 (Fig. 3(c)), which is consistent with its highest reaction conversion in catalytic tests (Table 1, entries 3–5). The lowest yield of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) for \( \mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66 \) results from deep penetration of Au NCs inside MOFs matrix and the restricted mass transport, which is consistent with the catalytic result (Fig. 3(c)). Compared with the two contrast samples, the generated amount of \( ^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) for the sandwich-like UiO-66@Au25@UiO-66 is moderate. Evidently, the reaction intermediate \( (^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}) \) might be regulated by structure optimization, thereby leading to tunable activity and selectivity.
In summary, three types of Au–UiO-66 nanocomposites with varied arrangement of Au NCs have been constructed by a coordinative and electrostatic interactions assisted self-assembly strategy. Impressively, as-prepared sandwich-like UiO\( 66@\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66 \) exhibits excellent catalytic performance in conversion, selectivity and cyclicity. A series of experiments including ESR test disclose that the sandwich-like structure not only improves the catalytic stability by strengthening immobilization of Au NCs but also remains high activity and selectivity through facilitating generation of interemediates \( (^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}) \). This work will open up the avenue towards design and construction of Au NC-MOFs nanocomposites as high-performance heterogeneous catalysts.
